\\gamma^{\\prime}_{\\rm gr}\\approx\\frac{\\omega^{\\prime}}{\\omega^{\\prime}_{p}} \\sim\\frac{5\\times 10^{3}\\,\\mu_{33}^{3/8}\\,\\nu_{9}^{5/8}}{\\tau_{\\rm sc}^{1/2} \\,[\\gamma_{p}(1+\\beta_{p})]^{3/16}R_{9}^{1/16}L_{42}^{9/32}},  where we substituted n_{0}=\\tau_{\\rm sc}/R\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc} and \\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}\\sim\\gamma^{\\prime 2}_{\\rm RRL}\\sigma_{\\rm T}. The huge \\gamma^{\\prime}_{\\rm gr} at \\tau_{\\rm sc}=1 shows that scattering of the wave is a much stronger effect than dispersion. (Strong dispersion of the wave packet would require time \\sim\\gamma^{\\prime}_{\\rm gr}T^{\\prime}, far exceeding the burst age in frame K^{\\prime}, t^{\\prime}\\sim R/\\gamma_{p}.) It also justifies the use of v^{\\prime}_{\\rm ph}=c in the calculation of \\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}.

## 3 Avalanche of pair creation

A magnetospheric particle exposed to the strong radio wave radiates the power {\\cal E}_{e}=\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}F^{\\prime} in photons with characteristic frequency \\omega^{\\prime}_{c}=(3/2)\\gamma^{\\prime 3}c/r^{\\prime}_{c}, where r^{\\prime-1}_{c}=(3\\dot{\\cal E}_{c}/2ce^{2}\\gamma^{\\prime 4})^{1/2} is the curvature of the particle's trajectory. Using the results of B21, we find in regime I:

\\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c}\\equiv\\frac{\\hbar\\omega^{\\prime}_{c}}{m_{e}c^{2}}\\approx \\,a_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{\\hbar\\omega^{\\prime}}{m_{e}c^{2}}\\approx\\frac{100}{\\gamma_ {p}(1+\\beta_{p})}\\frac{L_{3}^{3/2}}{R_{9}^{3}\\nu_{9}^{2}},  and in regime II:

\\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c}\\approx\\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\left(\\frac{r_{e}\\omega^{\\prime 2 }_{B}a_{0}}{c\\omega^{\\prime}}\\right)^{1/4}\\approx 44\\,\\frac{[\\gamma_{p}(1+\\beta_{p})]^{3/4} \\mu_{33}^{1/2}L_{42}^{1/8}}{R_{9}^{7/4}\\nu_{9}^{1/2}},  where \\alpha=e^{2}/\\hbar c. We conclude that the wave generates \\gamma-rays with \\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c}\\gg 1. Their spectrum has a peak with a half-width extending from 0.01\\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c} to 1.5\\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c} (e.g. Longair 2011). This broad peak extends over the MeV band where photon-photon collisions \\gamma+\\gamma\\to e^{+}+e^{-} occur with cross section \\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\gamma\\gamma}\\sim 0.1\\sigma_{\\rm T}.

It is convenient to view e^{\\pm} creation in frame K^{\\prime}, where the emitted \\gamma-rays are quasi-isotropic.3 The energy emitted as the wave expands to radius R, {\\cal E}^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}={\\cal E}_{\\rm sc}/\\gamma_{p} occupies radial thickness \\Delta R^{\\prime}\\sim R/\\gamma_{p}, so the \\gamma-rays have energy density

U^{\\prime}_{\\gamma}\\approx\\frac{\\tau_{\\rm sc}\\mathcal{E}}{4\\pi R^{3}}. 

They exit behind the wave on a timescale comparable to the wave duration T^{\\prime}=\\gamma_{p}(1+\\beta_{p})T, and a fraction of \\gamma-rays convert to e^{\\pm} pairs before exiting. The number of converting \\gamma-rays per primary particle is

\\mathcal{M}_{\\pm}=\\frac{\\bar{\\sigma}^{\\prime}_{\\gamma\\gamma}U^{\\prime}_{\\gamma }cT^{\\prime}}{m_{e}c^{2}}\\frac{\\dot{\\mathcal{E}}_{\\rm c}T^{\\prime}}{m_{e}c^{2} }\\approx\\zeta\\,\\frac{cT}{R}\\frac{\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}}{\\sigma_{\\rm T}}\\, \\ell^{2}\\,\\tau_{\\rm sc},  where

\\ell\\equiv\\frac{\\sigma_{\\rm T}\\mathcal{E}}{4\\pi R^{2}m_{e}c^{2}}\\approx 66\\, \\frac{\\mathcal{E}_{39}}{R_{9}^{2}},  and \\bar{\\sigma}^{\\prime}_{\\gamma\\gamma}=\\zeta\\,\\sigma_{\\rm T} is an effective cross section for \\gamma\\gamma collisions. The numerical factor \\zeta may be found by integrating the \\gamma\\gamma reaction rate over the spectrum of curvature emission. It depends on \\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c} and varies around \\zeta\\sim 10^{-2} at radii where \\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c}>1.

The e^{\\pm} created by \\gamma-rays oscillate in the wave and emit secondary \\gamma-rays just like the primary particles do. An avalanche of e^{\\pm} creation develops if \\mathcal{M}_{\\pm}\\gg 1, boosting the plasma density by e^{\\mathcal{M}_{\\pm}}. One can see that \\mathcal{M}_{\\pm} is large, even if one chooses a low density n_{0}=n_{\\rm co},

\\mathcal{M}_{\\pm}\\sim\\frac{10^{8}\\zeta}{R_{9}^{7}}\\;\\mathcal{E}_{39}^{2}\\,T_{- 3}\\,\\sigma_{8}^{2}\\,\\mathcal{N}_{32}\\gg 1,  where \\sigma_{8}=\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}/10^{8}\\sigma_{\\rm T}. The wave becomes immersed in the exponentially enriched e^{\\pm} plasma with \\tau_{\\rm sc}\\gg 1.

## 4 Discussion

We have found \\tau_{\\rm sc}\\gg 1 for FRBs emitted deep inside the magnetosphere and attempting to escape through the outer magnetosphere. This result is obtained for a given strong radio burst, not including the feedback of energy losses on its luminosity L. In reality the burst luminosity must drop to satisfy \\tau_{\\rm sc}\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\raise 2.1973pt\\hbox{$<$}}{\\lower 2.1973pt \\hbox{$\\sim$}}}1, respecting energy conservation \\mathcal{E}_{\\rm sc}\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\raise 2.1973pt\\hbox{$<$}}{ \\lower 2.1973pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}}\\mathcal{E}. Alternatively, the condition \\tau_{\\rm sc}(L,R)\\sim 1 may be used to define a minimum emission radius for a given L. Our results imply that observed FRBs cannot be released deep inside the magnetospheres of magnetars, at radii R\\ll 10^{10}\\,cm. This constraint is based on established laws of electrodynamics and should be robust.

The constraint should hold for FRBs with any temporal structure, including double FRBs. The first burst will accelerate and displace the outer magnetosphere outside R\\sim 10^{9}\\,cm (Eq. 11). Yet, this displacement creates no holes that could save the second burst from scattering -- it still must pass through the quasi-static magnetosphere just outside R_{0} and then through the expanding layers at R\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\raise 2.1973pt\\hbox{$>$}}{\\lower 2.1973pt\\hbox{$\\sim$ }}}10^{9}\\,cm. Note that the magnetospheric outflow launched by the scattering of the first FRB has a smooth radial profile: its speed increases from v_{p}\\approx 0 in the inner layers to v_{p}\\sim c in the outer layers, and such smooth outflows do not develop holes. In addition, the outflow will get loaded with e^{\\pm} plasma created by \\gamma-rays trailing the first FRB. Then, the perturbed magnetosphere will begin to relax toward a new equilibrium. Thus, the burst will fail to clear out an escape route and save subsequent FRBs from scattering.

Interestingly, lowering L may not help FRBs escape, because \\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}\\propto L^{-3/8}_grows_ at lower L (Eq. 20), and \\epsilon^{\\prime}_{c}\\propto L^{1/8} weakly changes. The main helping effect of lowering L is the growth of R_{0}\\propto L^{-1/4} (Fig. 1). When L\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\raise 2.1973pt\\hbox{$<$}}{\\lower 2.1973pt\\hbox{$\\sim$ }}}10^{36}\\,erg/s, R_{0} reaches \\sim 10^{10}\\,cm, the zone of strong particle acceleration 1<a_{0}<\\omega_{B}/\\omega disappears, and the wave scattering practically vanishes. The FRBs detected from SGR 1935+2154 are close to this transition.

We conclude that the scenario of a radio source confined in the inner magnetosphere is not satisfactory for FRBs in a broad range of observed luminosities. A remaining possibility for FRB emission by magnetars is a magnetic explosion -- the ejection of a large-scale electromagnetic pulse from the inner magnetosphere. The pulse has a size of 10^{7}-10^{8}\\,cm and is much more energetic than the observed GHz burst. Beloborodov (2017, 2020) proposed that the pulse drives an ultra-relativistic shock in the magnetar wind and the shock emits coherent radio waves by the synchrotron maser mechanism. A version of this model (a shock striking slow ion ejecta) was developed by Metzger et al. (2019). Alternatively, Lyubarsky (2014, 2020) argued that the ejected pulse will freely propagate through the magnetar wind, like a vacuum electromagnetic wave (this can happen under certain conditions which will be discussed elsewhere). The field in the pulse is expected to have small-scale perturbations, and they may be released at a large radius as escaping radio waves (Lyubarsky, 2020).

Calculations of wave scattering in this _Letter_ focused on the closed magnetosphere at R<R_{\\rm LC}. In a similar way, one can evaluate \\tau_{\\rm sc} in the wind at radii R\\gg R_{\\rm LC}, where n_{0}\\sim\\mathcal{N}/R_{\\rm LC}R^{2}, B_{\\rm kg}\\approx\\mu/R_{\\rm LC}^{2}R, and scattering occurs in regime I (\\omega^{\\prime}_{B}<\\omega^{\\prime}) with \\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\rm sc}\\approx a_{0}^{2}\\sigma_{\\rm T}. With increasing radius, \\hbar\\omega^{\\prime}_{c}\\approx a_{0}^{3}\\hbar\\omega^{\\prime} quickly falls below m_{e}c^{2}, so secondary e^{\\pm} creation becomes suppressed, and one finds

\\tau_{\\rm sc}\\sim\\frac{a_{0}^{2}\\sigma_{\\rm T}\\mathcal{N}}{R_{\\rm LC}R}\\approx \\frac{0.7\\,L_{42}\\mathcal{N}_{37}}{R_{10}^{3}\\nu_{9}^{2}(P/1\\,{\\rm s})}\\quad( R>10^{10}\\,{\\rm cm}). 

In particular, \\tau_{\\rm sc} is negligible if the observed FRBs are emitted by blast waves in the magnetar wind, which generate coherent radio emission at R\\sim 10^{13}-10^{14}\\,cm, although _induced_ scattering could still be important in this model (Beloborodov, 2020).

